Z||000000||SwampBat||05-28-99||06:28 PM||||Ok, after reading and looking and talking and listening and searching and scratching <br>my head, I come to this conclusion ... DOH!!!!!!! I guess I just need to know one thing, Is there any other motor (besides the one that came in the car) the will "just bolt up"? Say I got a '78 231 ci V6 out of a Grand Prix (78 was the first year the 6 was offered) would it bolt up? Ok lets say I got a CHEVY 262 4.3 V6 from '87, or a '74 V6 250 ci from a FireBird ('74 you say?) I want to take a motor and rebuild it and NOT have to <br>spend tons of $$ just to get it to go into the car, I am very confident that I can get the horse's I want out of any of these motors, what is the best and easiest engine to "just bolt in"? And what about the difference in 60deg and 90deg blocks?<br>awaiting your feedback .........  SwampBat
Z||000001||PaUL||05-28-99||08:04 PM||||Swamprat, Ill try to answer some of your questions. The 90deg chevy v6 are a completely different animal and will not bolt up to any fiero trans. As for the Buick 90deg v6s, all the ones made for FWD cars will fit, but I think you need a custom flywheel if you want to use a manual trans. <br>Ive seen some older model Buicks and Oldsmobiles with this engine and the trans bolt pattern looked the same but they came with auto trans only. 
Z||000002||SwampBat||05-28-99||08:27 PM||||I will be using the auto tranny. So your saying that most/all Fwd engines will match up? (does this mean the 3800 too) Then what is the biggest displacement engine on a fwd V6? I also found out that I can get a V8 fwd from a caddy, something like an 86/87 Eldo ..... but have you ever priced rebuild parts for a caddy motor? $$!!!
Z||000003||PaUL||05-28-99||10:24 PM||||The biggest displacement V6 for FWD must be the 3.8. I know that this engine has the same bolt pattern. The Caddi engines are V8s?<br>Ive seen the 90deg 3.8 in 80s buick centuries and olds cutlass supremes. Some of the later models came with a supercharger.
Z||000004||Vincent Whitright||06-02-99||07:42 PM||||Let me add to this discussion. A 3.8 will bolt up mechanically we all understand. But my question is as follows. Is the 3.1 the largest motor you can run and still use all the factory wiring...cheifly the ECM? Or can a 3.4 used with the factory Fiero ECM and wire harness?
Z||000005||Carrolles||06-03-99||08:15 AM||||Here is what I've read from various sources.<p>The 3.4 will work with the existing wiring harness and ECM.<br>The 3.4 (all cast iron rocker arm version) requires moving the starter to the other side of the motor.  The original starter area must be "rounded off" for transaxel clearance.  In addition, the modified 85-87 engine shock mount bracket needs to be used for lower a/c bracket attachment.  Use either a matched 85-86 oil pan/timing chain cover, or an 87-88 timing chain cover with the 3.4 pan.<p>Use an adjustable fuel pressure regulator set to 46-48 initial lbs with the 3.4 injectors.  On 88 cradles, use a modified 85-87 engine mount bracket.  Oil pressure sensor tube needs 1/4" pipe to 5/16" tubing adapter.  Don't forget that the 3.4 is internally balanced and will need an 88 V6 Fiero flexplate or flywheel.  It would be rather tricky to hand measure the new starter bolt holes.  Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory (fierofactory@juno.com) has a drill guide plate for this.
Z||000006||GREENDANO||06-03-99||04:14 PM||||One more thought, If your set on not making a 3.1/3.2 stock motor rebuild.  What about a supercharged 3800? There's a good deal of power there, just a little more work that unbolting and bolting parts.  A few companies make 3.8 swap conversions.  Look up Fiero stuff on the internet, there a quite a few.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000007||PaUL||06-04-99||02:10 AM||||Could you use a 3.4 block with stock fiero 2.8 heads? what would the resuting comp. ratio be? 
Z||000008||Vincent Whitright||06-04-99||07:34 AM||||Green, I was trying to find out what the largest motor I could "bolt" in ( that means with a small amount of modification), and still use the factory ECM. I don't think the 3800 can use the factory ECM. Also, I could still use just about all factory parts by using  at least a 3.1 that would fit the 2.8. I'm still researching the 3.4 and think it's the same family of engines whereas the 3800 isn't along with the 4.3.
Z||000009||PaUL||06-04-99||12:32 PM||||GM used 2 different V6s in their cars in the 80s. The 3.8 is a 90degree v6 and was made by Buick. The 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4 are all 60 degree v6s made by chevrolet. The later 3 are very similar and you could use the stock exhaust, ECM, wiring harness, etc. as is. On the 60 degree v6s, GM started using aluminum heads in the early 90s. These heads are different from the cast iron ones on the fiero, the intake port spacing is different so you wouldnt be able to use the fiero intake manifold with these heads. The 90 degree 3.8s would require lots of additional parts like exhaust and alternator brackets. Ive read that one of the bad things about the 60 degree engines is the small space between the heads. This necessitates an intake manifold with relativly short runners. The 90deg Buick with its cyclinder banks at a right angle to each other doesnt have this problem. BTW the 90deg buick was actually used and raced in Indycars in the 80s, although they were not very durable. 
Z||000010||SwampBat||06-06-99||10:29 AM||||Ok so if the 3800 is a 90deg motor, why not just go with a 4.1 252ci 90deg V6? They have been in use since '80 What I want to know is if the 3800 is 90deg and will bolt to the tranny, then shouldnt a 4.1? And if it will then how come noone has done aswap with a 4.1 (I havnt seen or heard of any) Also, will a crank from a 90deg motor work in the 60deg? If so then could you use a crank from a 4.3 with a stroke of 3.48" out of a gm motor?<br>maybe I will just say to hell with it all and put together a 700hp 500ci and squezzzzzzz it in my fiero! What Im trying to avoid is having to spend a lot of $$ on modifying lots of thing to get a motor to fit, If I was going to go that way I would just go with a V8, Im on a budget, so Im trying to fing the biggest block that will fit and go from there.<br>one more thing, is the 2.8 an even fire motor?
Z||000011||spike||06-07-99||07:01 AM||||I am also trying to find the biggest motor possible to fit into a Fiero. I have heard some bad things about a V8 swap, one guy told me his car did not handle well. I quess the idea is to keep it as light as possible. I have been considering many choices and I think that if I were to swap the engine it would be a 3.8. Does anyone know if this motor can be stroked. I was thinking of using one from a 87 vehicle. I have read that it could be set up with a Edelbrook intake and carb., this would eliminate any wiring hassels. The only problem is the HP rating for these engines. There must be a way to get some good reliable performance from this engine at a resonable price. Will the Heads from a GN 3.8 Turbo fit on the FWD 3.8 engine. I am not sure if some extra HP can be found in these heads. Will research more on subject.
Z||000012||PaUL||06-07-99||02:20 PM||||Im not sure about the 4.1. If its the same motor that was in the GMC Typhoon truck then it definitely will not bolt up. GM has 2 different 90 degree v6s, one produced by Buick the other by Chevy. The one that will bolt up is the FWD Buicks.
Z||000013||PaUL||06-07-99||02:24 PM||||Theres no way that the crank from the 3.8 will fit in any of the 60 degree v6s. The 3.8 crank is a split pin design, they made it this way so the engine would fire evenly. The older 3.8s produced in the 70s had a regular crank but fired unevenly resulting in a less than smooth idle. 
Z||000014||GREENDANO||06-07-99||08:39 PM||||As far as the biggest motor that will bolt up... It's the 3.4.  But, Get your wallet out.  Plus I've heard they're difficult to locate.  GM is being stingy.  I have a 3.2 in my 85 GT.  It's a stock block, borred 0.040 over with a camaro 3.1 crank.  It goes VERY WELL.  It makes just about 200 HP and It is very mean sounding.  I also have the IRM exhaust, with the big GM performance cam.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000015||Eric||06-07-99||08:59 PM||||Dano, did you do any porting and polishing?  How about oversized valves?  I plan on doing these thing when I rebuild my engine.  I may take some time with a Dremel, but I know it's worth it.
Z||000016||theogre||06-08-99||07:24 AM||||don't use a dremel for porting work. It won't accept the right size abrasives for the job. the small stones/disks dremel uses are just not suited for most of the work. even the larger abrasives it will take aren't the right ones.<p>If you do use a dremel in some of the tight places, make sure it's the variable speed one and take you time. It's really easy to dig a hole you don't want with this tool. Even in steel.
Z||000017||PaUL||06-08-99||01:17 PM||||Greendano, the 3.4 is biggest 60deg V6 that will bolt up to the fiero trans. The biggest would be the 90deg 3.8 from FWD cars like the Pont Bonneville and Buick Century.
Z||000018||GREENDANO||06-08-99||04:16 PM||||I don't want to get into a pissing match, but the question was which is the biggest motor that will bolt up without major mods or computer changes as I read it.  A 3.8 would require an ECM change as well as new/different motor mounts.<br>  So I'm sticking with my 3.4 answer.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000019||GREENDANO||06-08-99||04:52 PM||||Eric,<br> No porting to the heads or bigger valves.  I only gasket matched the intakes and did a little porting/cleaning.  The heads don't need any work as per the GM Performance engine building book.<br>  Dan-O
